{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01G6SeL6QfpWntMsNMbYtUeL","type":"tool_result","content":"204:You will nonetheless see \"60 questions, 75% to pass\" repeated across study sites, videos, and forum threads, stated flatly, as one fact. It is two facts with two pedigrees: a passing standard the certifying body publishes, and a question count that travels on repetition alone.\n211:> A study plan built on \"60 questions in 90 minutes\" yields a pacing rule of 90 seconds per question, and that rule feels precise and authoritative. If the real count is different, the rule is wrong, and you find that out at minute forty of a ninety-minute exam, with a proctor watching and no way to reset.\n341:- **A is wrong.** The question count is not on the exam page — or anywhere else the certifying body writes. \"60 questions\" is a third-party report, repeated so consistently that it reads as official. It isn't.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d388d963-c143-4111-9729-fe2603d659a3","uuid":"9b7f693b-cc59-4ea9-957a-5d7a1ea009e8","timestamp":"2026-08-31T22:33:13.291Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"204:You will nonetheless see \"60 questions, 75% to pass\" repeated across study sites, videos, and forum threads, stated flatly, as one fact. It is two facts with two pedigrees: a passing standard the certifying body publishes, and a question count that travels on repetition alone.\n211:> A study plan built on \"60 questions in 90 minutes\" yields a pacing rule of 90 seconds per question, and that rule feels precise and authoritative. If the real count is different, the rule is wrong, and you find that out at minute forty of a ninety-minute exam, with a proctor watching and no way to reset.\n341:- **A is wrong.** The question count is not on the exam page — or anywhere else the certifying body writes. \"60 questions\" is a third-party report, repeated so consistently that it reads as official. It isn't.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
